1
00:00:00,165 --> 00:00:01,835
<u>Scalability & Capacity</u>

2
00:00:03,166 --> 00:00:04,156
<i>What is scalability?</i>

3
00:00:05,410 --> 00:00:09,030
Scalability is the ability of a system to handle growth in users, traffic, data,

4
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:11,980
or work without unacceptable loss of quality or cost efficiency.

5
00:00:13,497 --> 00:00:14,687
<i>What is capacity planning?</i>

6
00:00:16,198 --> 00:00:18,728
Capacity planning estimates when a resource will reach a limit.

7
00:00:19,428 --> 00:00:23,768
Its purpose is to identify likely constraints early, not to predict every production number exactly.

8
00:00:25,273 --> 00:00:26,203
<i>What is RPS?</i>

9
00:00:27,739 --> 00:00:31,799
RPS (Requests Per Second) is the number of requests received or processed per second.

10
00:00:32,509 --> 00:00:36,859
It is useful, but it does not describe payload size, work cost, concurrent connections, storage growth,

11
00:00:37,029 --> 00:00:37,949
or external quotas.

12
00:00:39,478 --> 00:00:41,168
<i>Why is average RPS not enough?</i>

13
00:00:42,683 --> 00:00:47,493
Average traffic hides peaks, regional concentration, launches, retries, bursts, and expensive endpoints.

14
00:00:48,193 --> 00:00:51,723
Capacity should be estimated for peak critical paths, not only daily averages.

15
00:00:53,270 --> 00:00:54,260
<i>What is concurrency?</i>

16
00:00:55,803 --> 00:00:59,163
Concurrency is the number of operations or connections in progress at the same time.

17
00:00:59,873 --> 00:01:03,743
A system can have moderate RPS but still be stressed by many long-lived or slow requests.

18
00:01:05,257 --> 00:01:06,347
<i>What is vertical scaling?</i>

19
00:01:07,862 --> 00:01:11,422
Vertical scaling gives one node more CPU, memory, or I/O capacity.

20
00:01:12,132 --> 00:01:16,982
It is simple, but one machine has limits and remains a single failure domain unless redundancy is added separately.

21
00:01:18,497 --> 00:01:19,777
<i>What is horizontal scaling?</i>

22
00:01:21,294 --> 00:01:23,004
Horizontal scaling adds more nodes.

23
00:01:23,714 --> 00:01:28,324
It can increase capacity and redundancy, but requires routing, coordination, deployment, observability,

24
00:01:28,494 --> 00:01:30,034
and state management across instances.

25
00:01:31,905 --> 00:01:33,235
<i>What does stateless service mean?</i>

26
00:01:34,490 --> 00:01:37,170
A stateless service keeps durable request state outside the process,

27
00:01:37,400 --> 00:01:39,700
so requests can be routed to interchangeable instances.

28
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:44,730
Temporary in-memory state may still exist, but correctness must not depend on one specific instance.

29
00:01:46,225 --> 00:01:47,295
<i>What is a load balancer?</i>

30
00:01:48,801 --> 00:01:51,671
A load balancer distributes traffic across multiple instances.

31
00:01:52,381 --> 00:01:54,221
It can improve utilization and availability,

32
00:01:54,441 --> 00:01:57,531
but it does not remove downstream bottlenecks such as an overloaded database.

33
00:01:59,057 --> 00:01:59,977
<i>What is a bottleneck?</i>

34
00:02:01,494 --> 00:02:04,984
A bottleneck is the resource that currently limits system performance or capacity.

35
00:02:05,694 --> 00:02:11,414
It may be CPU, database writes, a hot partition, a lock, a connection pool, an external quota, or a slow network path.

36
00:02:12,945 --> 00:02:13,935
<i>What is a hotspot?</i>

37
00:02:15,459 --> 00:02:19,539
A hotspot is a disproportionately busy key, partition, tenant, region, or resource.

38
00:02:20,249 --> 00:02:24,059
Total system capacity can look healthy while one hotspot causes latency or failures.

39
00:02:25,585 --> 00:02:26,535
<i>What is backpressure?</i>

40
00:02:28,075 --> 00:02:33,005
Backpressure is a mechanism that limits, delays, or rejects incoming work when downstream capacity is exhausted.

41
00:02:33,705 --> 00:02:37,035
It prevents unbounded queues and gives overloaded components a chance to recover.

42
00:02:38,542 --> 00:02:40,582
<i>How can mobile clients create traffic spikes?</i>

43
00:02:42,102 --> 00:02:45,742
Polling intervals, reconnect storms after connectivity returns, push-triggered refreshes,

44
00:02:45,892 --> 00:02:47,742
app launches after notifications, retries,

45
00:02:47,932 --> 00:02:51,632
and long-lived realtime connections can create bursts that DAU and average RPS do not reveal.
